[agent] | Type | cached |
[agent] | **Online** | **yes** (low-latency/real-time lookup enabled, distinct from offline) |
[agent] | Primary key | `row_id` |
[agent] | Event time | `event_time` (epoch ms, `bigint`) |
[agent] | Schema | exactly `row_id` (string), `account_id` (string), `event_time` (bigint), `amount_usd` (double), `is_weekend` (bigint), `amount_7d` (double) |
[agent] **How the work ran entirely platform-side:**
[agent] 1. Loaded raw `transactions.csv` (700 rows) and `fx_rates.csv` (4 rows) into offline feature groups via `hops fg insert`.
[agent] 2. Computed all derived columns in **Trino on the platform** (no local compute):
[agent]    - `amount_usd = amount * fx_rate` via a join to the fx feature group;
[agent]    - `is_weekend` via UTC-safe epoch arithmetic — `floor(event_time/86400000) % 7 IN (2,3)` (day 0 = Thursday ⇒ Sat/Sun), avoiding any session-timezone ambiguity;
[agent]    - `amount_7d` via a per-account window `SUM(amount) OVER (PARTITION BY account_id ORDER BY event_time RANGE BETWEEN 604800000 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW)` — inclusive 7-day window on both ends.
[agent] 3. Materialized the Trino result into the online+offline feature group via `hops fg insert` (default insert writes both stores).
[agent] Spot-checks confirmed correctness (e.g. A0000: GBP 8.52 → amount_usd 10.8204, first-in-window amount_7d 8.52; consecutive rows within 7 days sum to 89.73; rows >7 days apart reset).
[agent] **One CLI sharp edge worth flagging** (not a blocker, worked around platform-side): `hops fg insert` with a `.json` file routes through `pandas.read_json`, whose default `convert_dates=True` silently coerces any column named `*_time` (here `event_time`) into a timestamp and widens integers to bigint — which rejected the insert against a `bigint`/`int` schema. The `.csv` path (`read_csv`) does not do this, so I emitted the Trino result as CSV instead. An explicit type/no-date-coercion option on `hops fg insert` would remove this footgun.
